Brute Force Solution (Count Nodes):

Approach:
Traverse the linked list and count the total number of nodes.
Find the middle position using count / 2 + 1.
Traverse the linked list again until the middle position.
Return the middle node.

Time Complexity: O(n)
One traversal to count the nodes.
One traversal to reach the middle node.
Overall: O(n)

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra data structure is used.

Code: 
class Solution {
public:
    // Function to get the middle node of linked list
    ListNode* middleOfLinkedList(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* temp = head;
        int count = 0;
        
        // Count the total nodes
        while(temp != NULL) {
            count++;
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        
        int midPosition = (count / 2) + 1;
        
        // Move to the middle node
        ListNode* middleNode = head;
        for(int i = 1; i < midPosition; i++) {
            middleNode = middleNode->next;
        }
        
        return middleNode;
    }
};

Optimal Solution (Slow and Fast Pointer)
Approach:
Initialize two pointers, slow and fast, at the head.
Move slow one step and fast two steps in each iteration.
When fast reaches the end, slow will be at the middle node.
Return the slow pointer.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Traverse the linked list once.

Space Complexity: O(1)
Only two pointers are used.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    // Function to get the middle node of linked list
    ListNode* middleOfLinkedList(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != NULL && fast->next != NULL) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        return slow;
    }
};
